  Enhancing the Competency of using problem solving strategy in mathematics among senior secondary teachers in Sri Lanka
]

= 1 Introduction

== 1.1 Background of the study

Mathematics education plays a pivotal role in shaping the analytical and critical thinking skills of students, which are essential for their success in an increasingly complex world. In Sri Lanka, the quality of mathematics instruction at the senior secondary level is crucial, as it lays the foundation for students' future academic and professional pursuits. However, many educators face challenges in effectively teaching mathematics, particularly in employing problem-solving strategies that foster deeper understanding and engagement among students.

Problem-solving is not merely a skill but a fundamental aspect of mathematical learning that encourages students to explore, conjecture, and reason. It empowers them to approach mathematical concepts with confidence and creativity. Despite its importance, research indicates that many senior secondary teachers in Sri Lanka may lack the necessary competencies to effectively implement problem-solving strategies in their classrooms. This gap can lead to a less engaging learning environment and hinder students' ability to develop critical mathematical skills.

This research aims to enhance the competency of senior secondary mathematics teachers in Sri Lanka by focusing on effective problem-solving strategies. Through this research, we hope to illuminate the pathways for enhancing pedagogical practices and nurturing a generation of students who are proficient in mathematics and prepared for the challenges of the future.

Mathematics teaching provides students with the ability of critical thinking and problem solving. The effective teaching of mathematics is critical for developing students' problem-solving skills, which are essential for their academic success and future careers. The implementation of problem-solving strategies in mathematics classrooms can be significantly influenced by teachers. Many teachers face challenges in employing problem-solving strategies that foster deeper understanding and engagement among students.

Teachers have to upgrade their knowledge in enhancing problem-solving strategies in secondary mathematics teaching in Sri Lanka to meet the challenges in the modern mathematical world.

The latest international studies and some cognitive research studies suggest that there is a space for the improvement in the mathematics knowledge of the teachers (ICMI, 2016). A number of studies report that teachers’ mathematics content knowledge is insufficient, leaving them underprepared to teach in ways that help students internalize salient concepts (Siregar, 2017). In Sri Lanka, the National Education Commission (2018) report says that teachers who are appointed to teach mathematics for students fail to upgrade their knowledge. Department of Examination (2019) report also states that the performance of teachers in mathematics is poor.

== 1.2 Research Problem

In Sri Lanka, senior secondary mathematics teachers face significant challenges particularly in the effective implementation of problem-solving strategies. Although many teachers possess subject knowledge in mathematics they struggle to integrate problem-solving techniques into their teaching practices. As such a lack of student engagement and poor performance in mathematics is evident. This gap in pedagogical skills not only affects students' critical thinking abilities but also hinders the ability of teachers to effectively teach and impart mathematical skills to students. Thus, there is an urgent need to enhance the competency of senior secondary teachers in using problem-solving strategies to foster a more interactive and effective learning environment, ultimately improving student outcomes in mathematics across the region. This study aims to investigate and enhance the competency of senior secondary teachers in using problem-solving strategies in mathematics education.
